  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 242 PM EST Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Model consensus continues to favor a coastal type\nlow to deepen and wrap moisture back across Vermont and northern\nNew York Thursday with a more upslope regime developing by\nFriday. Temperatures and this pattern support lower elevation\nrain Thursday and mainly higher elevation snowfall before\ntemperatures drop Thursday night promoting snow showers to most\nvalley floors. The overall forecast remains quite tricky with\nbalancing QPF timing, changing SLRs, and a shift in primary\nprecipitation mechanisms while models struggle with the details.\n\nWhere the models continue to struggle are placement of the\nsurface circulation, extent of northward moisture transport and\nQPF amounts, and amount of cold air in place across the region.\nThe latest trends have pushed moisture back southward leaving\nmore modest QPF across northern New York and Vermont\nrepresenting nearly a 50% reduction to QPF from the previous\nforecast for many locations. As such, snow totals were decreased\nwith most spots only seeing a dusting to around 1 inch, southern\nand Adirondack locations below 1200 feet receiving 1-2 inches,\nand 2-5 inches above 1200ft. This will primarily be a function\nof the period of heaviest precipitation period occurring over \nthe daytime hours with snow ratios being lower due to thermal \nprofiles. As such, snow will start at higher elevations and \ncrash down to around 1000 feet over the evening hours as the low\npulls away. The upslope regime begins Thursday night and \ncontinues through early Friday with higher snow ratios \nsupporting more efficient accumulation rates, but hindered by \ndecreasing moisture. Cooling profiles and dynamic cooling \nprocesses will help snow reach most valley locations overnight \nThursday night and early Friday. However, it will be tough to \nget snow accumulations in lower valleys like the Champlain \nValley given the relatively warm ground temperatures likely \nmelting most, if not all, of the snow that reaches the ground \nThursday night. For the St Lawrence Valley, most of the moisture\nwill have exited before temperature profiles cool enough to \nallow snow to occur. \n\nBehind the coastal low, northerly flow will remain through\nSaturday keeping shower chances ongoing, especially across\nterrain. One \"gotcha\" to watch out for will be the potential for\nstreamers to form off of Lake Champlain producing some higher\nelevation snow showers. Temperatures over the weekend may be\ncooler as well depending on cloud cover which may keep highs\nlower. Valley highs over the weekend are favored in the lower\n40s, but could decrease into the upper 30s if clouds proliferate\noff of Lake Champlain given continued cool air advection. Lows\nwill be in the 20s for most spots. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2:  The departing low will continue to pull away from the \nregion Sunday, leading to continued northwesterly wrap-around flow \nwith a few lingering showers.  As the low exits, the depth of \nmoisture will decrease throughout the day and shower coverage will \nfurther decrease from Saturday. Lower elevations will be mostly dry, \nwhile higher elevations Sunday will still see a few showers. \nThroughout the weekend, precipitation type will be elevation-\ndependent; snow levels will be generally between 1000 and 1500 ft. \nHigher elevations will pick up up to an additional inch over the \nweekend, with limited depth of moisture being the main limiting \nfactor in larger wrap-around snow accumulations. Lower elevations \nshould not expect any accumulating snow. \n\nMonday onward, the forecast turns very quiet with high confidence in \nhigh pressure building in and larger diurnal temperature swings. \nHighs will be near seasonable normals, but lows will be chillier \nthan normal, especially going into midweek.",
